aquinas-st-i-1.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r2 has "person_signifies_distinct ∧ human_person_flesh_bones_soul"; aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r2.3 have "person_signifies_distinct"; they differ when only ""person" in any nature signifies what is distinct in that nature" is true, which the second allows
aquinas-st-i-1.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps aquinas-st-i-1.logic.r2, to: Suppose ""person" in any nature signifies what is distinct in that nature" is true and "in human nature "person" signifies this flesh, these bones and this soul, the individuating principles of a man, which belong to the meaning of a particular human person though not to "person" in general" is false. Is the conclusion, "Therefore "person" in any nature signifies what is distinct in that nature: thus in human nature it signifies this flesh, these bones, and this soul, which are the individuating principles of a man, and which, though not belonging to "person" in general, nevertheless do belong to the meaning of a particular human person.", true then?
